    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "It's kind of sad to see. It feels like we're moving away from art that's actually beautiful or transcendent and just moving toward art that functions like a lecture or a pamphlet. If the goal is just to make a point, why do we even need the \"art\" part of it? Isn't the whole point of art to be something more than just a political tool?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "This perspective raises questions about the fundamental purpose of art and its role in society. When art is reduced solely to a political statement, it loses its ability to evoke emotions, spark intuition, and tap into the human experience in a deeper way. Art should, in theory, transcend its message and speak to something fundamental about the human condition. The fusion of art and politics can result in a reduction of art to a mere propaganda tool, stripping it of its transformative potential and lasting impact on the viewer.",
